\details{
Detection of marker feats using the
\href{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gini_coefficient}{gini}
coefficient is based on the following steps/principles, per feat:
\enumerate{
\item \code{expression}: the mean expression over the cells of each cluster.
\item \code{detection}: the fraction of each cluster's cells whose expression
exceeds \code{detection_threshold}.
\item \code{expression_gini}: the gini coefficient of the \code{expression} values.
\item \code{detection_gini}: the gini coefficient of the \code{detection} values.
\item \code{expression_rank} / \code{detection_rank}: the clusters ranked by \code{expression}
and by \code{detection}, rank 1 being the cluster where the feature is
highest. These are ranks of the \strong{values}, not of the gini
coefficients, and are what \code{rank_score} filters on. Ties share a rank.
\item \code{comb_score}: the same two orderings are rescaled within each cluster to
[1, 0.1] to act as weights, and \code{comb_score} = \code{expression_gini} x
expression weight x \code{detection_gini} x detection weight. The weights are
internal and are not the returned rank columns: they resolve ties by
averaging where step 5 resolves them by minimum, so on a tied feature
\code{comb_score} cannot be reproduced from the returned ranks alone.
\item within each cluster, sort by \code{comb_score} (descending) and number the
result as \code{comb_rank}.
}

Steps 1 and 2 reduce the matrix to two features x clusters tables; every
step after that is arithmetic on those tables. So the vector handed to the
gini calculation in steps 3 and 4 holds \strong{one value per cluster}, not one
per cell — its length is the number of clusters being compared. Two
consequences worth knowing before setting \code{min_expression_gini} or
\code{min_detection_gini}:
\itemize{
\item a gini coefficient over \code{G} values cannot exceed \code{(G - 1) / G}, so the
scale depends on how many clusters are being compared: the ceiling is
0.95 for 20 clusters, 0.80 for 5, and 0.50 for 2. A threshold chosen for
a many-cluster run is therefore not transferable to a smaller one, where
it may sit above the ceiling and reject every feature;
\item \code{\link[=findGiniMarkers_one_vs_all]{findGiniMarkers_one_vs_all()}} compares each cluster against all others
pooled into a single group, so its coefficients are always taken over
exactly two values and are capped at 0.50 — well below what the same
feature would score in a multi-cluster run here.
}

\code{min_length} exists to remove that dependence; see the \emph{Comparing runs}
section below.

As a result "top gini" feats are feats that are very selectively expressed
in a specific cluster,
however not always expressed in all cells of that cluster. In other words
highly specific, but
not necessarily sensitive at the single-cell level.
}
\section{Filtering}{

Two kinds of gate are available, and they do different jobs.

\code{min_expression} and \code{min_detection} are an abundance floor. The gini
coefficient is scale-free: a feature averaging 0.001 in one cluster against
0.0001 in the rest scores exactly as specific as one averaging 100 against
10. It therefore carries no magnitude term, and will rank near-noise
features as perfectly selective. These two gates supply that term. The
equivalent for the other methods is \code{logFC}, which
\code{\link[=findScranMarkers_one_vs_all]{findScranMarkers_one_vs_all()}} and \code{\link[=findMastMarkers_one_vs_all]{findMastMarkers_one_vs_all()}} filter
on directly.

\code{min_expression_gini} and \code{min_detection_gini} gate the gini coefficients
themselves, and are off by default. They are best used on a second pass:
the returned table always carries the \code{expression_gini} and \code{detection_gini}
columns, so run once, look at their distribution, then set a floor. What
they add over \code{min_feats} is an \emph{absolute} cutoff, where \code{min_feats} is a
\emph{relative} one -- it takes each cluster's top few by \code{comb_score} however
weak those are, while a gini floor is a statement about the coefficient
itself.

\code{rank_score} is a third kind: a position rather than a value. It keeps a
feature when the cluster in question is within the given rank for both
\code{expression} and \code{detection}, rank 1 being the cluster where the feature is
highest. So \code{rank_score = 1} means "this cluster tops the feature on both
measures", and \code{2} means "top two". It is off by default (\code{Inf}). Clusters
tied at the top all hold the same rank, so a tie does not exclude them.

All four value comparisons are strict (\code{>}), so a value exactly equal to
its threshold does not pass; \code{rank_score} is inclusive (\code{<=}). The strict
case is worth knowing for \code{min_detection}, where the values are fractions
over a cluster's cell count and land on round numbers often.

Every gate is combined with \code{min_feats} by \code{or}, so a feature in the top
\code{min_feats} of its cluster by \code{comb_score} is kept regardless of all of
them. Setting the gates more strictly therefore shrinks the result towards
\code{min_feats} features per cluster, never below it.
}

\section{Comparing runs}{

A \emph{run} here means one call together with the set of clusters that call
compares — \code{G} of them. A gini coefficient over \code{G} values cannot exceed
\code{(G - 1) / G}, so a coefficient is a property of the feature \strong{and} of the
run that produced it, not of the feature alone.

\code{G} is not fixed by the dataset; it is set by the arguments. On the mini
visium object with seven leiden clusters:\tabular{llll}{
   call \tab \code{G} \tab ceiling \tab highest \code{expression_gini} \cr
   \code{findGiniMarkers()} \tab 7 \tab 0.857 \tab 0.484 \cr
   \verb{findGiniMarkers(subset_clusters = 3 of them)} \tab 3 \tab 0.667 \tab 0.443 \cr
   \code{findGiniMarkers(group_1 =, group_2 =)} \tab 2 \tab 0.500 \tab 0.390 \cr
   \code{findGiniMarkers_one_vs_all()} \tab 2 \tab 0.500 \tab 0.309 \cr
}


The feature \code{Mustn1} scores 0.484 in the full run and 0.238 in the
three-cluster subset — the same cells, the same expression, half the
coefficient, because \code{subset_clusters} changed what it was compared against.
That is the boundary: any point where a coefficient produced under one \code{G}
is read against one produced under another.

\code{min_length} pads the per-cluster vector with copies of its own minimum
before the coefficient is taken, fixing the ceiling at
\code{(min_length - 1) / min_length} for any run with fewer clusters than that,
and so putting runs with different \code{G} back on one scale.

Padding is not a neutral rescaling, and it is not strictly better than
leaving it off. Two things follow from the padding value being \emph{each
feature's own minimum}. It reorders features within a single run, because a
feature with a floor of zero gains far more from padding than one with a
high floor — measured on mini visium, padded and unpadded
\code{expression_gini} correlate at 0.95, not 1.0. And it asserts something the
data does not contain: \code{c(10, 0)} scores 0.50 over two groups and 0.9375
padded to sixteen, which amounts to assuming the feature would sit at its
observed minimum in fourteen groups nobody measured. Being top of two
clusters really is weaker evidence of specificity than being top of twenty;
the unpadded ceiling is that fact, not an artefact.

So this is a deliberate trade — a comparable number in exchange for an
assumption — and it cannot be chosen automatically, because the right value
depends on which \emph{other} runs the score has to line up with, which a single
call cannot see.

\strong{Leave it at \code{0} when} you are reading one run's results on their own
terms — the default, and the case that needs no assumption. Nothing in the
returned table requires an absolute coefficient: \code{comb_rank}, \code{min_feats}
and \code{rank_score} are all relative to the run, and the gini columns are only
being read against each other.

\strong{Set it when a gini number has to mean the same thing twice.} In rough
order of how easily each is hit:
\itemize{
\item comparing a full run against one narrowed by \code{subset_clusters}, or against
a \code{group_1}/\code{group_2} pairwise call — the same object and the same
clustering, but a different \code{G}, so the coefficients are not on one scale;
\item comparing \code{\link[=findGiniMarkers]{findGiniMarkers()}} against \code{\link[=findGiniMarkers_one_vs_all]{findGiniMarkers_one_vs_all()}},
whose two-group comparisons are capped at 0.50 while a 20-cluster run
reaches 0.95;
\item reusing one \code{min_expression_gini} or \code{min_detection_gini} threshold across
clustering resolutions, or across datasets, where the cluster count
differs;
\item reporting a coefficient as a property of a feature rather than of one
analysis.
}

All four are the same situation: a coefficient produced under one \code{G} being
read against one produced under another.

Pick a value at least as large as the biggest cluster count you want to
compare across. Below that, a run with more clusters than \code{min_length} is
left unpadded and the scales still differ. Above it there is no real cost:
padding harder widens the range rather than compressing it, and barely
touches the ordering (measured on the mini visium dataset, \code{min_length} of
16 versus 100 gives a Spearman correlation of 0.998 between the resulting
coefficients). \code{16} is the value this padding shipped with historically and
fixes the ceiling at 0.9375.

Turning it on is not free. Every gini score changes, so results stop being
comparable with unpadded runs — pad both sides or neither. And since
\code{comb_score} is built from the coefficients, the reordering described above
propagates: on mini visium \code{min_length = 16} moves \code{comb_rank} for 98\% of
rows, which changes which features \code{min_feats} rescues and therefore which
features are returned at all.

To perform differential expression between custom selected groups of cells
you need to specify the cell_ID column to parameter \emph{cluster_column}
and provide the individual cell IDs to the parameters \emph{group_1} and
\emph{group_2}

By default group names will be created by pasting the different id names
within each selected group.
When you have many different ids in a single group
it is recommend to provide names for both groups to \emph{group_1_name} and
\emph{group_2_name}
